.TH TATAMI 8 "" "tatami"
.SH NAME
tatami \- package manager with dependency resolution and atomic transactions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tatami
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B tatami keygen
.RB [ \-f ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B tatami sign
.I file
.RI [ keyfile ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tatami
installs, upgrades and removes binary packages.
It is built on the design of pkgutils: a plain\-text package database, packages
that are tar archives, and a separate tool,
.BR tatami\-mkpkg (8),
that builds them.
Dependencies are resolved by libsolv; archives are read and written by libarchive.
The libraries are built into the program (linked as static archives), so no external
program is needed to handle any of the supported compression formats (gzip, zstd,
xz, lzma, bzip2, lz4).
.PP
Packages are named
.IB name _ version [\- release ]_ arch . extension
for example
.BR zlib_1.3.2\-1_x86_64.tar.pkg .
The extension is
.B tar.pkg
by default (see
.BR tatami.conf (5));
the compression inside is detected from the content.
Everything tatami reads or writes is below the installation root
.RB ( \-\-root ),
which is
.B /
by default.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B update
Prepare the repositories listed in
.BR tatami.conf (5):
write the
.B repo.solv
index of each local repository (it holds the metadata and the SHA\-256 checksum of every package),
and download the index of each remote one.
.TP
.BI install " package" "|" file ...
Install packages and the dependencies they need, or upgrade them if they are
installed.
.I package
is a name, optionally
.IB name = version\fR;
.I file
is a package file, which is installed directly.
Packages that are installed only as dependencies are marked as such.
.TP
.BI remove " package" ...
Remove packages, everything that depends on them, and the dependencies that are
then no longer needed by a package that was installed explicitly.
There is no separate autoremove command.
.TP
.B upgrade
Upgrade all installed packages that have a newer version available.
Packages named in
.B /etc/tatami/hold
are skipped, each with a notice.
.TP
.BI search " regex"
Search names and descriptions in the repository indexes.
.TP
.BI show " package" ...
Show details of a package: version, architecture, relations, size, file,
SHA\-256 checksum, and whether it is installed (and why).
.TP
.BR list " [" \fIregex\fP ]
List installed packages; with
.B \-\-upgradable
those that have a newer version, with
.B \-\-available
the packages of the repositories instead.
.TP
.BI files " package" "|" file
List the files of an installed package or of a package file.
.TP
.BI owns " regex"
Find the installed package that owns files matching the pattern.
.TP
.BR keygen " [" \-f "] [" \fIfile\fP ]
Make an Ed25519 key pair.
The private key is written to
.I file
(default
.B $TATAMI_PRIVKEY
or
.BR ~/.tatami/privkey ,
mode 0600), the public key to
.IR file .pub,
and the command that copies the public key into
.B /etc/tatami/keys/
on the client machines is printed.
An existing key is replaced only with
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BI sign " file " [ keyfile ]
Write
.IR file .sig,
the Ed25519 signature of a file, with the key
.I keyfile
(default as for
.BR keygen ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
Without a terminal tatami refuses to continue unless this is given.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite files that belong to another package or that already exist.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-simulate
Show what would be done and change nothing.
Commands that modify the system then need no root privileges.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-root " " \fIpath\fP
Use
.I path
as the installation root.
The database, journal, configuration, repositories, caches, hooks and the
extraction all live below it.
Symbolic links inside the root, including absolute ones and a merged
.B /lib \-> usr/lib
layout, are followed as if the root were
.BR / ,
so nothing is ever written outside of it.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIfile\fP
Read this configuration file instead of
.BR <root>/etc/tatami/tatami.conf .
It must exist.
.TP
.B \-\-installed, \-\-upgradable, \-\-available
Select what
.B list
shows.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help and exit.
.PP
Commands that modify the system can only be run by root.
.SH TRANSACTIONS
Everything one command does \- all the installations, upgrades and removals of its
plan \- is a single transaction: it is applied completely or not at all.
.PP
Before anything is extracted tatami adds up the unpacked size of the incoming
packages and refuses to continue if the free space of the root's filesystem,
with a small safety margin, would not be enough.
Remote packages are downloaded and verified first.
.PP
Every change the extraction makes is recorded in an undo journal,
.BR <root>/var/lib/tatami/transaction.journal ,
before it is made: files and directories that are created, files that are
overwritten (moved aside in their own directory), and directory modes.
The package database is only changed in memory, and removed files are not deleted yet.
.PP
When every package has been extracted the deletions are journalled, the journal gets
a commit record, the database is written with an atomic rename, and only then are the
old files and the backups removed.
The packages that were downloaded for the transaction are deleted from the cache.
.PP
If anything fails, in any package, tatami rolls back every package of the run:
created files are removed, overwritten files restored, and the database,
which was never written, is reloaded.
The downloaded packages stay in the cache for a retry.
.PP
If tatami is killed (even with SIGKILL) or the machine loses power during a
transaction, the next command that modifies the system reads the journal first.
Without a commit record the whole run is rolled back; with one, the database
decides: if it shows the result of every operation the run is finished,
otherwise it is rolled back.
.PP
Before asking, a command that installs, upgrades or removes prints, when standard
output is a terminal, a summary in the style of FreeBSD's pkg:
the packages grouped under
.B "New packages to be INSTALLED:"
(the ones named on the command line),
.BR "Dependencies to be INSTALLED:" " (pulled in to satisfy requirements)" ,
.BR "Packages to be UPGRADED:" " (" "name: old -> new" ),
.B "Packages to be DOWNGRADED:"
and
.BR "Packages to be REMOVED:" ,
the number of packages in each group (for installs with the split, \fB(Y requested, Z dependencies)\fR), how much space the process will require or free,
and how much is to be downloaded, and then asks
.BR "Proceed with this action? [Y/n]:" .
Nothing in this output uses colours or escape codes.
When the output is not a terminal (a pipe or a log) the plain lists and the one-line
count are printed instead, so scripts see a stable format.
The size to be extracted of a remote package comes from its index; it is stored in
.B repo.solv
when a repository is indexed (a package whose index lacks it counts as 0).
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
A file that an
.B UPGRADE
rule of
.B /etc/tatami/upgrade.rules
(see
.BR tatami.conf (5))
says
.B NO
to is treated as a configuration file (by default everything below
.BR etc/ ).
When an upgrade brings a version that differs from the file on disk, the new file is
installed and the existing one is renamed to
.IB file .old
\- for example
.BR /etc/foo.conf.old .
An identical file is left alone and an earlier
.B .old
file is replaced.
The rename is part of the transaction: a rollback puts the old file back.
.SH SECURITY
Packages and indexes can be signed with Ed25519 (RFC 8032, through libsodium).
.B tatami\-mkpkg
signs what it builds;
.B tatami
verifies what it downloads.
.PP
For a
.I remote
repository
.B update
fetches
.B repo.solv
and
.BR repo.solv.sig ,
and
.B install
fetches each package and its
.BR .sig .
Every file is verified with the trusted public keys, the files in
.B /etc/tatami/keys/
(a signature by any one of them is good), before anything is stored or installed; a missing, malformed or wrong signature
aborts the command.
Cached indexes are verified again each time they are loaded, and cached packages are
reused only if their signature still verifies.
To trust a signer, copy its
.B .pub
file into that directory; to stop, delete the file.
Without a key there a remote repository is refused, unless
.B ALLOW_UNSIGNED = yes
in
.BR tatami.conf (5)
says that unsigned data is acceptable.
.PP
The index also contains the SHA\-256 checksum of every package.
A package downloaded from a remote repository is hashed right after the download and
must match the index, so a file substituted on the server is detected; a
mismatch discards the download.
.PP
.I Local
sources are trusted: a package file given on the command line, a repository given as
a path or as a
.B file://
URL, are never checked for a signature or a checksum and need no key.
.PP
Downloads are made by the command
.B FETCHCMD
of the configuration file, which is run directly (not through a shell).
On a terminal its progress output is replaced by tatami's own bar, in plain text like
pacman's:
.RS
.nf
sm-a_1.0_x86_64.tar.pkg   [##########....................]  33%
.fi
.RE
tatami itself contains no network code.
.SH INSTALL SCRIPTS
A package built with a
.B tatami.install
file (see
.BR tatami\-mkpkg (8))
contains the script as the hidden member
.BR .INSTALL ,
which is never installed as a file.
Its function
.B pre_install
runs before the package's files are extracted and
.B post_install
after the transaction was committed.
Both receive the package version as
.B $1
and run with
.B TATAMI_ROOT
set to the installation root.
If
.B pre_install
fails the transaction is rolled back; a failing
.B post_install
is only reported.
The scripts run as root on the host, not chrooted.
.SH HOOKS
Files
.B <root>/etc/tatami/hooks.d/*.hook
describe what to run after a transaction, such as
.BR ldconfig (8).
See
.BR tatami.conf (5).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/tatami.conf
Configuration.
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/hold, /etc/tatami/upgrade.rules
Held packages and file rules.
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/repos.conf, /etc/tatami/repos.d/*.conf
Repositories.
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/hooks.d/*.hook
Post\-transaction hooks.
.TP
.B /var/lib/tatami/db
The package database; next to it are
.B meta/
(metadata and install reasons of the installed packages) and
.BR transaction.journal .
.TP
.B /var/lib/tatami/packages
The default local repository.
.TP
.B /var/lib/tatami/repos/\fIname\fB/
Downloaded indexes of remote repositories.
.TP
.B /var/cache/tatami/packages
Downloaded packages, until their transaction commits.
.TP
.B ~/.tatami/privkey
The default signing key.
.PP
All paths except the key are relative to the installation root. The directories can
be changed when tatami is built (see the README).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TATAMI_PRIVKEY
Private key used by
.B sign
and
.BR keygen .
.TP
.B TATAMI_ROOT
Set by tatami for install scripts and hooks.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success, non\-zero if the command failed. A failed transaction changes nothing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tatami\-mkpkg (8),
.BR tatami.conf (5)
